{
  "abstract": "A set {w,x,y,z} is a regular quadruple when (w+x-y-z)^2 = 4(1+wx)(1+yz). Paper 164 shows that a rational Diophantine septuple contains at most six regular quadruples, and Paper 168 lists the 219 admissible arrangements of six and decides four of them. We prove by hand that if three regular quadruples share a pair {p,q} and cover a triangle {x,y,z} of distinct numbers, then pq = -3/4 and R(x,y,z,0) = 0, so the triangle is a regular triple with at most one nonzero regular fourth. Consequently the configuration of five blocks consisting of such a pair-triangle and two further blocks through the triangle has no point with seven distinct nonzero coordinates in any field of characteristic 0; an explicit identity over Q with cofactors of degree at most 4 certifies this. This decides ledger row 230160 and gives a second proof for orbit 10 using five of its six blocks. For row 692864 we give an explicit identity over Q expressing a product of six differences as a combination of its six block equations, so that row is decided as well; saturation over Q shows that no five of its blocks suffice. With these, 8 of the 219 arrangements are decided. Among the 88 admissible five-block configurations, the configuration above is the only one whose saturated ideal is the unit ideal modulo 1000003, and the same saturation of six-block rows finished on 206 of the 219 and gives the unit ideal only for 230160, 692864 and orbit 10. None of the proofs uses the 21 square conditions. The hand proof and all identity checks were rerun from the text by the coauthor.",
